# Initially generated by Claude.
"""Cross-repo health check over Claude Code's per-repo auto-memory directories.

Same shape as check_repo_standards.py: independent, best-effort, REPORT-ONLY
checks. Nothing here is auto-fixed, and a repo with no memory directory is
reported, not treated as an error.

The drain standard
------------------
AUTO-MEMORY IS A WIPEABLE CACHE, SO THE MAINTENANCE OPERATION IS DRAINING ITS
DURABLE CONTENT INTO THE TRACKED REPO -- NOT TIDYING THE CACHE. The files live
under ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/, outside every repo, untracked, on one
machine. Ben, recorded in wlc-utils' azla-names-either-mark memory: "I don't
have a way of persisting memory files off this machine, so they should be
viewed as temporary files that can be wiped at any time."

So the question to ask of each memory is not "is this tidy" but "is this
finding also in the tree?" -- yes, shrink it to a pointer; no and it is
durable, write it into the repo FIRST, then shrink. Pruning is the byproduct.
That is what makes this a repo-maintenance concern at all: its real output is
repo commits, which is also why this file can only ever report. Whether a
memory's content has reached the tree is a judgment no scan can make.

It is already happening organically, which is the evidence the model is right.
wlc-utils' repo-maintenance-vs-check-repo-standards memory records Ben's
2026-07-01 canonical_terms idea, noting the wording standards "exist today only
as Claude Code auto-memory, not checked-in anywhere" -- and wlc-utils'
test_prose_conventions.py has since grown to lint exactly those terms over py/,
gh-pages/*.html and out/**/*.json. Several memories were drained without anyone
naming the operation.

What this file checks, then, is only the decidable residue:
  - index_drift: MEMORY.md is the index loaded into EVERY session, so it is the
    one file with a recurring context cost. A memory file with no pointer is
    invisible; a pointer to a deleted file is a dead link.
  - frontmatter: an empty or missing `name:`/`description:`, or a `name:` that
    disagrees with the filename. `description:` is what a future session reads
    to decide relevance, so a blank one silently removes the memory from recall
    while leaving the file on disk. Three of wlc-utils' 77 files were in that
    state when this check was first written.
  - dangling_links: a [[double-bracket]] link naming no existing memory. Worse
    than cosmetic -- one of wlc-utils' three pointed at a memory whose
    conclusion had since been REVERSED, so following it would have restored a
    retired rule.
  - stale_cited_paths: a repo-relative path cited in a memory that exists in no
    repo in the sweep. See "Resolve citations against every repo" below.

    READ A HIT AS "CHECK THIS", NEVER "FIX THIS" -- most hits are correct. On the
    first cross-repo run, 9 of the 11 surviving hits were legitimate citations of
    a path that is *supposed* to be absent, in four distinct flavours:
      * PLANNED BUT NOT BUILT -- UXLC-utils' gh-pages/clc/differences.html, the v1
        artifact of a filed issue with no code written yet.
      * DELIBERATELY RETIRED -- UXLC-utils' tools/dump_mam_*.py, throwaway
        scaffolding whose own memory says it dies once the issue is encoded. Its
        absence CONFIRMS that memory rather than dating it.
      * NAMED IN ORDER TO BE REJECTED -- UXLC-utils' py/sitecustomize.py, which Ben
        refused and which therefore never existed. This is the same distinction Ben
        drew about wlc-utils issue #13's `munah`/`tarha` spellings  # translit-ok
        -- a naive scan cannot tell a neutral mention from one that promotes a
        thing from one that names it to reject it.  (That trailing pragma is the
        same point in miniature: this line names the retired spellings in order
        to reject them, and test_transliterations cannot tell that either.)
      * GENERATED ON DEMAND -- wlc-utils' out/accgram/poetic/_bang_legality.txt,
        written by bang_legality.py when run and absent otherwise.
    The two genuine finds were both renames: breuer-cos' scripts/check_ch9_claims.py
    to scripts/check_cos_claims.py, and UXLC-utils' doc/clc-brainstorm.md to
    doc/clc-design.md (commit b9316f4), the latter having also left a dangling
    memory link behind it. A rename is what this check is really for.
  - size: file count and total KB, plus MEMORY.md's own line count and KB.
    Reported, deliberately unjudged -- see "No threshold" below.

Resolve citations against every repo
------------------------------------
A memory's cited paths must be resolved against ALL repos in the sweep, not
against the repo that owns the memory. The reference-type memories exist
precisely to point at OTHER repos -- wlc-utils' ctr-prior-art-in-mam-basics
cites py/diff_ctr_vs_mam/massage_ctr_verse.py in MAM-basics, and
claim-verification-precedent-mam-basics cites py/mb_author/claim.py there.
A first cut of this check resolved every citation against wlc-utils alone and
reported 27 stale paths, of which essentially all were that false positive: the
files were fine, in the repo the memory was telling you to look in. A path is
therefore a finding only when it is found in NO known repo, and the report names
where each resolved path was found, so a citation drifting to a different repo
is visible rather than silently passing.

The slug is case-inconsistent, so match case-insensitively
----------------------------------------------------------
The project directory is the repo's absolute path with ':' and the separators
replaced by '-'. But the drive letter's case is NOT stable: as of 2026-07-31
al-hatorah, MAM-basics, UXLC-utils and wlc-utils sit under a lowercase
'c--Users-...' while breuer-cos, holman-ketiv-qere, MAM-simple and
masorah-books sit under an uppercase 'C--Users-...'. Both spellings are live at
once, so computing the slug and doing an exact lookup finds roughly half of them.

Keying on the absolute path also means a RENAMED repo orphans its memory. When
yeivin-itm became masorah-books on 2026-07-31, its four memory files stayed at
'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos-yeivin-itm' under a name nothing now looks up, while
the new 'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos-masorah-books' started empty. That example has
since resolved and this paragraph used to claim otherwise: checked 2026-08-07,
'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos-yeivin-itm' holds four session transcripts and NO
memory subdirectory at all, and masorah-books carries 23 memory files of its
own.

'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos-breuer-cos' is the worked example of why an orphaned
memory directory is a QUESTION rather than an instruction to move the files. It
holds one memory file, 'wlc-koren-12th-is-a-worktree.md', which is about
wlc-utils rather than about Breuer, and which wlc-utils' own
'no-wlc-koren-12th-repo.md' supersedes: that one was written 21 minutes later on
2026-07-27, records that the directory it names was deleted, and corrects this
one by name. Carrying that file into masorah-books, as the obvious reading of
"the repo's work moved there" suggests, would plant a superseded memory in a
repo it was never about. So read an orphaned memory before moving it.

That directory is now beyond this check's reach, and that is the part worth
knowing. Ben's decision, 2026-08-07: the breuer-cos CLONE was deleted from
~/GitRepos, and breuer-cos was dropped from all-repos.code-workspace and from
in/repo_maintenance_policy.json's frozen_repos, its content having lived in
masorah-books since 2026-07-31. This check only walks the repos a workspace file
lists, so 'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos-breuer-cos\\memory' still sits on disk while
nothing reports it any more. Deleting a repo, or dropping one from a workspace
file, is therefore the one way to orphan a memory directory SILENTLY -- look at
the memory before doing either.

Two neighbours in that directory are deliberately NOT repo memory and are
skipped: 'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos' itself (sessions started from the GitRepos
parent rather than inside a repo), and the per-worktree slugs
'<repo>--claude-worktrees-<id>', which are agent worktrees. The worktree ones
are counted and reported as `stale_worktree_project_dirs` because they
accumulate exactly like the worktrees themselves do -- wlc-utils had 13 with
zero surviving worktrees -- and they are the same litter
check_repo_standards.py's worktree_hygiene check exists to surface.

No threshold
------------
Size is reported, never judged. A count or a KB ceiling would be a made-up
number, and the thing that actually costs something is MEMORY.md's per-session
load, which a human reading the number can weigh better than a constant can.
This mirrors why black_pin was removed from check_repo_standards.py: a check
that fires identically on every run against a settled preference is noise.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import re
from pathlib import Path

from repo_util.common import write_json, write_text
from repo_util.repo_selection import RepoInfo

_CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR = Path.home() / ".claude" / "projects"

_MEMORY_SUBDIR = "memory"
_INDEX_NAME = "MEMORY.md"

# A per-worktree project slug, e.g.
# 'C--Users-BenDe-GitRepos-wlc-utils--claude-worktrees-sleepy-bell-ad6a3e'.
_WORKTREE_SLUG_MARKER = "--claude-worktrees-"

_WIKILINK_PATTERN = re.compile(r"\[\[([^\]|]+)")

_INDEX_LINK_PATTERN = re.compile(r"\]\(([^)]+\.md)\)")

# Frontmatter is a small YAML block; these two fields are read with a line regex
# rather than a YAML parser, matching this repo's heuristic-text-scan style.
_NAME_FIELD_PATTERN = re.compile(r"^name:\s*(.*)$", re.MULTILINE)
_DESCRIPTION_FIELD_PATTERN = re.compile(r"^description:\s*(.*)$", re.MULTILINE)

# A repo-relative path mentioned in memory prose. Anchored on the top-level
# directory names these repos actually use, so ordinary prose containing a slash
# is not mistaken for a path.
#
# The lookbehind is load-bearing: \b alone matches mid-token after a hyphen, so
# '../MAM-with-doc/gh-pages/misc/x.html' yielded a phantom 'doc/gh-pages/misc/x.html'
# that exists nowhere and was reported as stale. Requiring the character before
# the match to be neither a word character, a hyphen, nor a slash means a path is
# only ever recognised from its true start.
_REPO_PATH_PATTERN = re.compile(
    r"(?<![\w/-])((?:py|doc|in|out|gh-pages|tools|scripts)/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.[a-z]{2,10})\b"
)

# Citations that are illustrative rather than literal -- an elided path
# ('out/.../_printed_decalogue.json') or a glob -- name no single file, so
# checking them for existence is meaningless.
_UNCHECKABLE_PATH_MARKERS = ("...", "*", "<", ">")


def _expected_slug(repo_dir: Path) -> str:
    """The project-directory name for a repo path: ':' and both separators
    become '-'. Case is NOT reliable in the real directory (see the module
    docstring), so callers must match case-insensitively."""
    return str(repo_dir).replace(":", "-").replace("\\", "-").replace("/", "-")


def _find_memory_dir(repo_dir: Path) -> Path | None:
    """Locate a repo's memory directory, matching the slug case-insensitively
    and ignoring the per-worktree slugs."""
    if not _CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR.is_dir():
        return None
    wanted = _expected_slug(repo_dir).casefold()
    for child in _CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR.iterdir():
        if not child.is_dir():
            continue
        if _WORKTREE_SLUG_MARKER in child.name:
            continue
        if child.name.casefold() != wanted:
            continue
        memory_dir = child / _MEMORY_SUBDIR
        return memory_dir if memory_dir.is_dir() else None
    return None


def _count_worktree_project_dirs(repo_dir: Path) -> int:
    """How many per-worktree project directories this repo has left behind."""
    if not _CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR.is_dir():
        return 0
    prefix = (_expected_slug(repo_dir) + _WORKTREE_SLUG_MARKER).casefold()
    return len(
        [
            child
            for child in _CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR.iterdir()
            if child.is_dir() and child.name.casefold().startswith(prefix)
        ]
    )


def _memory_files(memory_dir: Path) -> list[Path]:
    return sorted(p for p in memory_dir.glob("*.md") if p.name != _INDEX_NAME)


def _frontmatter(text: str) -> str:
    if not text.startswith("---"):
        return ""
    end = text.find("\n---", 3)
    return text[:end] if end != -1 else ""


def _field(pattern: re.Pattern[str], frontmatter: str) -> str | None:
    match = pattern.search(frontmatter)
    if match is None:
        return None
    value = match.group(1).strip().strip('"').strip("'")
    return value or None


def _check_index_drift(memory_dir: Path, files: list[Path]) -> dict:
    index_path = memory_dir / _INDEX_NAME
    if not index_path.is_file():
        return {
            "index_present": False,
            "unindexed_files": [p.stem for p in files],
            "index_points_at_missing": [],
        }
    index_text = index_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    indexed = {Path(m).stem for m in _INDEX_LINK_PATTERN.findall(index_text)}
    stems = {p.stem for p in files}
    return {
        "index_present": True,
        "unindexed_files": sorted(stems - indexed),
        "index_points_at_missing": sorted(indexed - stems),
    }


def _check_frontmatter(files: list[Path]) -> list[str]:
    findings: list[str] = []
    for path in files:
        frontmatter = _frontmatter(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        name = _field(_NAME_FIELD_PATTERN, frontmatter)
        description = _field(_DESCRIPTION_FIELD_PATTERN, frontmatter)
        if name is None:
            findings.append(f"{path.name}: empty or missing name")
        elif name != path.stem:
            findings.append(f"{path.name}: name '{name}' disagrees with filename")
        if description is None:
            findings.append(f"{path.name}: empty or missing description")
    return findings


def _check_dangling_links(files: list[Path]) -> list[str]:
    stems = {p.stem for p in files}
    findings: list[str] = []
    for path in files:
        targets = sorted(
            set(_WIKILINK_PATTERN.findall(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")))
        )
        findings.extend(f"{path.name} -> {t}" for t in targets if t not in stems)
    return findings


def _check_cited_paths(
    files: list[Path], repo_dirs: list[Path], own_repo_name: str
) -> dict:
    """Cited repo-relative paths that exist in NO repo in the sweep, plus the
    ones that resolve ONLY outside the memory's own repo. See "Resolve citations
    against every repo" in the module docstring.

    A path that resolves in the owning repo is silent even when a same-named file
    exists elsewhere too -- `py/tests/test_transliterations.py` is not a
    cross-repo citation just because several repos have a file by that name.
    """
    stale: list[str] = []
    elsewhere: list[str] = []
    for path in files:
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        for cited in sorted(set(_REPO_PATH_PATTERN.findall(text))):
            if any(marker in cited for marker in _UNCHECKABLE_PATH_MARKERS):
                continue
            homes = [d.name for d in repo_dirs if (d / cited).exists()]
            if not homes:
                stale.append(f"{path.name} -> {cited}")
            elif own_repo_name not in homes:
                elsewhere.append(f"{path.name} -> {cited} (in {', '.join(homes)})")
    return {"stale_cited_paths": stale, "cited_paths_in_other_repos": elsewhere}


def _dir_size_kb(paths: list[Path]) -> float:
    return round(sum(p.stat().st_size for p in paths) / 1024, 1)


def _check_one_repo(repo_dir: Path, repo_dirs: list[Path]) -> dict:
    memory_dir = _find_memory_dir(repo_dir)
    worktree_dirs = _count_worktree_project_dirs(repo_dir)
    if memory_dir is None:
        return {
            "memory_present": False,
            "memory_dir": None,
            "stale_worktree_project_dirs": worktree_dirs,
        }

    files = _memory_files(memory_dir)
    index_path = memory_dir / _INDEX_NAME
    index_lines = 0
    index_kb = 0.0
    if index_path.is_file():
        index_lines = len(index_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines())
        index_kb = round(index_path.stat().st_size / 1024, 1)

    result = {
        "memory_present": True,
        "memory_dir": str(memory_dir),
        "file_count": len(files),
        "total_kb": _dir_size_kb(files),
        "index_lines": index_lines,
        "index_kb": index_kb,
        "stale_worktree_project_dirs": worktree_dirs,
        "frontmatter_findings": _check_frontmatter(files),
        "dangling_link_findings": _check_dangling_links(files),
    }
    result.update(_check_index_drift(memory_dir, files))
    result.update(_check_cited_paths(files, repo_dirs, repo_dir.name))
    return result


def _render_txt_report(results: list[dict]) -> str:
    lines: list[str] = []
    for repo in results:
        lines.append(f"=== {repo['repo']} ===")
        if not repo["memory_present"]:
            lines.append("memory_present: False")
            lines.append(
                f"stale_worktree_project_dirs: {repo['stale_worktree_project_dirs']}"
            )
            lines.append("")
            continue
        lines.append(f"memory_dir: {repo['memory_dir']}")
        lines.append(f"file_count: {repo['file_count']}  total_kb: {repo['total_kb']}")
        lines.append(
            f"index: {repo['index_lines']} lines, {repo['index_kb']} KB"
            " (loaded every session)"
        )
        lines.append(
            f"stale_worktree_project_dirs: {repo['stale_worktree_project_dirs']}"
        )
        for key, label in (
            ("unindexed_files", "files with no MEMORY.md pointer"),
            ("index_points_at_missing", "MEMORY.md pointers to missing files"),
            ("frontmatter_findings", "frontmatter"),
            ("dangling_link_findings", "dangling links"),
            ("stale_cited_paths", "cited paths found in no repo"),
            ("cited_paths_in_other_repos", "cited paths resolving in another repo"),
        ):
            findings = repo.get(key) or []
            if findings:
                lines.append(f"{label}:")
                lines.extend(f"  - {finding}" for finding in findings)
        lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def run_check_memory_health_across_repos(
    repo_infos: list[RepoInfo],
    *,
    report_json: Path | None,
    report_txt: Path | None,
) -> list[dict]:
    repo_dirs = [info.path for info in repo_infos]
    results: list[dict] = []

    for repo_info in repo_infos:
        results.append(
            {"repo": repo_info.name, **_check_one_repo(repo_info.path, repo_dirs)}
        )

    if report_json is not None:
        write_json(report_json, results)
    if report_txt is not None:
        write_text(report_txt, _render_txt_report(results))

    print(f"REPO_COUNT={len(results)}")
    for repo in results:
        if not repo["memory_present"]:
            print(
                "REPO={0}; MEMORY=False; WORKTREE_PROJECT_DIRS={1}".format(
                    repo["repo"], repo["stale_worktree_project_dirs"]
                )
            )
            continue
        print(
            "REPO={0}; MEMORY=True; FILES={1}; TOTAL_KB={2}; INDEX_LINES={3}; "
            "INDEX_KB={4}; UNINDEXED={5}; DEAD_POINTERS={6}; FRONTMATTER={7}; "
            "DANGLING_LINKS={8}; STALE_PATHS={9}; WORKTREE_PROJECT_DIRS={10}".format(
                repo["repo"],
                repo["file_count"],
                repo["total_kb"],
                repo["index_lines"],
                repo["index_kb"],
                len(repo["unindexed_files"]),
                len(repo["index_points_at_missing"]),
                len(repo["frontmatter_findings"]),
                len(repo["dangling_link_findings"]),
                len(repo["stale_cited_paths"]),
                repo["stale_worktree_project_dirs"],
            )
        )

    return results
